block.text:
Now Fix 2 also mentions "怎么回血，怎么使用物品，怎么穿戴装备？疗伤按钮呢？调养呢？" — we've added 疗伤, 调养, 行囊 buttons. The 行囊 panel handles item use and equipment. Good.

But the user also mentioned there should be 疗伤按钮 and 调养 buttons — already there in the side nav (btn-heal). Now we have dedicated quick buttons.

Now Fix 3: 地图的连线太乱了 — rearrange all maps to have straight lines, boxes connected flat to boxes (like a MUD grid map), not lines everywhere.

The current map uses hand-placed coordinates (CHENG_POS) with diagonal lines connecting them. The user wants a grid layout: rooms arranged in a grid, connections only orthogonal (horizontal/vertical), so it looks like a MUD map.

Approach: Instead of using the hand-placed coordinates for rendering, compute a grid layout algorithmically from the room graph (exits), and draw lines as orthogonal (Manhattan) paths between boxes.

This is the biggest task. Let me think about the design:

1. For each layer (zhou: cities; cheng: rooms in a city; nei: rooms in a building), build a graph from the exits.
2. Compute a layered layout:
   - BFS from the entry room to assign depth (column).
   - Within each depth, order nodes to minimize crossings.
   - Assign grid coordinates: x = depth * spacing, y = index * spacing.
3. Draw nodes as rounded rectangles (boxes) instead of circles — "方框平着连着方框".
4. Draw edges as orthogonal lines (horizontal then vertical, L-shaped or S-shaped) between box edges.

But wait — the user says "要求直线，方框平着连着方框，而不是现在这样哪里都有线条" — straight lines, boxes connected flat to boxes, not lines everywhere. So a grid layout with orthogonal edges.

The challenge: CHENG_POS data is huge (hundreds of rooms with hand-placed coordinates). If I change the renderer to compute layout algorithmically, I don't need to touch CHENG_POS data at all — I just ignore it during rendering and compute positions from the graph. But CHENG_POS is also used in:
- `mapSize()` for nei bounds (line 4745) — uses CHENG_POS to compute bounding box.
- `mapZoomReset()` (line 4808) — uses CHENG_POS to center on player room.
- `mapLocate()` (line 4823) — same.
- `renderChengMap()` (line 5316) — positions.

I should introduce a unified `layerPos()` function that computes a grid layout for the current layer (zhou/cheng/nei) and returns {pos: {room: {x,y}}, w, h}. Then use it everywhere instead of CHENG_POS.

Let me design the layout algorithm:

```
function computeLayerLayout(){
  const lv=game.mapLevel||'zhou';
  // collect nodes & edges
  let nodes=[], edges=[];
  if(lv==='zhou'){
    nodes = Object.keys(CHENG_CFG);
    edges = CHENG_LINKS;
  } else {
    const cid=game.cheng;
    const isNei=(lv==='nei');
    for(const rn in ROOMS){
      const r=ROOMS[rn];
      if(isNei){ if(r.bld===game.bld) nodes.push(rn); }
      else { if(r.area===cid && typeof r.bld!=='string') nodes.push(rn); }
    }
    const set={}; nodes.forEach(n=>set[n]=1);
    const seen={};
    nodes.forEach(rn=>{
      const r=ROOMS[rn]; if(!r||!r.exits) return;
      for(const d in r.exits){
        const t=r.exits[d];
        if(!set[t]) continue;
        const k=[rn,t].sort().join('|');
        if(seen[k]) continue; seen[k]=1;
        edges.push([rn,t]);
      }
    });
  }
  // BFS from root to assign columns
  const root = (nodes.indexOf(player.room)>-1) ? player.room : (lv==='zhou' ? 'cheng_lizhu' : (nodes[0]||''));
  ...
}
```

Hmm, but "方框平着连着方框" (boxes flat-connected) suggests a grid where most edges connect horizontally between adjacent boxes. The BFS-layered approach with columns = BFS depth gives a left-to-right flow, and edges go between adjacent columns mostly — but with the S-shape connectors, it looks clean.

Actually a simpler and very MUD-like approach: assign each room a grid cell (row, col) based on BFS depth, then draw all edges as orthogonal polylines (horizontal line out of box, vertical line, horizontal line into box). This is the classic "orthogonal layout" used in network diagrams.

To minimize crossings, order nodes within each column by their average neighbor position (barycenter heuristic), iterating a few times.

Let me write the algorithm:

```js
let _layerLayoutCache=null;
function layerLayoutKey(){ return (game.mapLevel||'zhou')+'|'+(game.cheng||'')+'|'+(game.bld||''); }
function getLayerLayout(){
  const key=layerLayoutKey();
  if(_layerLayoutCache && _layerLayoutCache.key===key) return _layerLayoutCache;
  const lv=game.mapLevel||'zhou';
  // --- nodes & edges ---
  let nodes=[], edgePairs=[];
  if(lv==='zhou'){
    nodes=Object.keys(CHENG_CFG);
    edgePairs=CHENG_LINKS.slice();
  } else {
    const cid=game.cheng, isNei=(lv==='nei');
    for(const rn in ROOMS){
      const r=ROOMS[rn];
      if(isNei){ if(r.bld===game.bld) nodes.push(rn); }
      else if(r.area===cid && typeof r.bld!=='string') nodes.push(rn);
    }
    const set={}; nodes.forEach(n=>set[n]=1);
    const seen={};
    nodes.forEach(rn=>{
      const r=ROOMS[rn]; if(!r||!r.exits) return;
      for(const d in r.exits){
        const t=r.exits[d];
        if(!set[t]||t===rn) continue;
        const k=[rn,t].sort().join('|');
        if(seen[k]) continue; seen[k]=1;
        edgePairs.push([rn,t]);
      }
    });
  }
  // --- adjacency ---
  const adj={}; nodes.forEach(n=>adj[n]=[]);
  edgePairs.forEach(p=>{ adj[p[0]].push(p[1]); adj[p[1]].push(p[0]); });
  // --- BFS depth from root ---
  const idx={}; nodes.forEach((n,i)=>idx[n]=i);
  const root = (idx[player.room]!==undefined)?player.room:(lv==='zhou'?(CHENG_CFG['cheng_lizhu']?'cheng_lizhu':nodes[0]):(nodes[0]||''));
  const depth={}; nodes.forEach(n=>depth[n]=-1);
  if(root!==undefined&&root!==''){
    depth[root]=0;
    const q=[root];
    while(q.length){
      const cur=q.shift();
      (adj[cur]||[]).forEach(nb=>{ if(depth[nb]===-1){ depth[nb]=depth[cur]+1; q.push(nb); } });
    }
  }
  // disconnected → append to last column
  let maxD=0; nodes.forEach(n=>{ if(depth[n]>maxD) maxD=depth[n]; });
  nodes.forEach(n=>{ if(depth[n]===-1){ maxD++; depth[n]=maxD; } });
  // --- group by depth ---
  const cols={}; nodes.forEach(n=>{ (cols[depth[n]]=cols[depth[n]]||[]).push(n); });
  const ds=Object.keys(cols).map(Number).sort((a,b)=>a-b);
  // order within column: barycenter heuristic
  const order={}; // n -> row index
  ds.forEach(d=>{ cols[d].sort(); cols[d].forEach((n,i)=>order[n]=i); });
  for(let pass=0; pass<6; pass++){
    // forward pass
    for(let ci=1; ci<ds.length; ci++){
      const d=ds[ci];
      cols[d].forEach(n=>{
        const nbs=(adj[n]||[]).filter(x=>depth[x]===ds[ci-1]);
        if(nbs.length) order[n]=nbs.reduce((s,x)=>s+order[x],0)/nbs.length;
      });
      cols[d].sort((a,b)=>order[a]-order[b]);
      cols[d].forEach((n,i)=>order[n]=i);
    }
    // backward pass
    for(let ci=ds.length-2; ci>=0; ci--){
      const d=ds[ci];
      cols[d].forEach(n=>{
        const nbs=(adj[n]||[]).filter(x=>depth[x]===ds[ci+1]);
        if(nbs.length) order[n]=nbs.reduce((s,x)=>s+order[x],0)/nbs.length;
      });
      cols[d].sort((a,b)=>order[a]-order[b]);
      cols[d].forEach((n,i)=>order[n]=i);
    }
  }
  // normalize rows to consecutive ints
  ds.forEach(d=>{ cols[d].sort((a,b)=>order[a]-order[b]); cols[d].forEach((n,i)=>order[n]=i); });
  // --- coordinates ---
  const isZhou = lv==='zhou';
  const cellW = isZhou?300:250;
  const cellH = isZhou?200:170;
  const pad = isZhou?160:140;
  const bw = isZhou?210:180; // box width
  const bh = isZhou?90:78;   // box height
  const pos={};
  let maxRows=0;
  ds.forEach((d,ci)=>{
    const col=cols[d];
    maxRows=Math.max(maxRows,col.length);
    const colH=(col.length-1)*cellH;
    col.forEach((n,ri)=>{
      pos[n]={ x: pad+ci*cellW, y: pad + colH/2 + ri*cellH };
    });
  });
  const w = pad*2 + (ds.length-1)*cellW;
  const h = pad*2 + (maxRows-1)*cellH;
  const res={ key, pos, w, h, bw, bh, lv };
  _layerLayoutCache=res;
  return res;
}
```

Wait, but there's an issue with using BFS depth columns for the zhou map: cities are connected by CHENG_LINKS and the graph may have cycles, so BFS depth works fine as a layering.

But there's a subtlety: some cheng rooms' exits point to rooms that are NOT in the current layer (e.g., interior rooms excluded). We filter with set. Good.

Also for the zhou layer, the "return gate" node is drawn at (0,0). Keep that.

Now rendering:
- Draw edges as orthogonal paths: from box center to box center with an "S" or "Z" bend at midpoints.
- Draw nodes as rounded rects with labels inside.

Let me write the orthogonal edge path:
```js
function edgePath(x1,y1,x2,y2, bw, bh){
  // start/end at box edges
